<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papacy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as papal, Pope</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The papacy is the office of the Pope, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and head of the Catholic Church. In the eighth and ninth centuries it allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>, culminating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s coronation in 800, and later crowned the Holy Roman emperors. Popes also proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> against Muslim powers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, and sought alliances with the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papacy.jpg" alt="Papacy" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgar Beltrán, The Pillar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Leo_XIV_3_(3x4_cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
